const HTML = "<p>I used to think religion was a fairly obvious con. A priest could tell you what God wanted, threaten you with punishment after death, and claim the authority to explain how to avoid it, while God&#39;s wishes seemed remarkably well suited to the men speaking on his behalf. Believing them struck me as gullible, and I liked to think I was harder to fool.</p>\n<p>In my thirties I began reading religious texts with less contempt. Revelation gives you plenty to laugh at if that&#39;s what you&#39;ve come for, with its scarlet beast with seven heads, its woman dressed in purple, its jewels and golden cup. The book does not make much effort to accommodate the reader who would prefer a tasteful metaphor.</p>\n<p>The woman represents a city, where kings have dealings with her and merchants grow rich from her luxury, and when the city falls they weep because nobody will buy their goods. The list of merchandise runs through gold, linen, spices, livestock, and ends with human beings. People are being sold, and the men watching the city burn are mourning the market. <a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Revelation%2017-18&amp;version=KJV\">Revelation 17–18</a></p>\n<p>I had thought of religious control mainly as something imposed on people: obey or be punished. Babylon offers things a person can want quite sincerely: wealth, influence, the company of people whose approval makes life easier. A warning about corruption is more useful to me when I can recognise something I would hate to lose.</p>\n<p>Suppose a group of parents is raising money for their children&#39;s school and one of them proposes posting the names of families who haven&#39;t contributed. They have all spent evenings organising the fundraiser, and some are annoyed that the same parents seem to do all the work. A public list would embarrass people into paying. That is the proposal, however kindly someone introduces it.</p>\n<p>Another parent objects. Nobody knows what those families can afford, and some may already be ashamed of it. The objection needn&#39;t settle the matter; it ought at least to make the organisers hesitate. But suppose the answer is that the money is for the children, that the volunteers are exhausted, and that anyone criticising the plan should have volunteered earlier. Now the discussion is about the objector&#39;s contribution. The families whose names will be published have disappeared from it.</p>\n<p>No one has needed to stop caring about children. Caring has become the reason they give for humiliating some of their parents. If a respected organiser endorses the list, everyone who wanted it acquires another reason to feel comfortable. Someone sensible has approved, so they can get on with it.</p>\n<p>I don&#39;t find the figure of the false prophet particularly antiquated when I read it this way. A person who can give cruelty a respectable purpose has something valuable to offer, because they spare others the discomfort of wanting it for themselves.</p>\n<p>In Stanley Milgram&#39;s obedience experiment, published in 1963, volunteers were told to administer increasingly severe electric shocks as part of a study of learning. The recipient was acting, so no shocks were delivered to him, but the volunteers were being asked to proceed as though they were real.</p>\n<p>Twenty-six of forty continued to the highest setting. Fourteen stopped.</p>\n<p>The fourteen who stopped had been given the same instructions. Refusal was possible. Milgram also recorded trembling, sweating and stuttering; some participants were visibly distressed while doing what they had been instructed to do. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1037/h0040525\">Milgram, 1963</a></p>\n<p>Their distress makes the experiment harder to dismiss, because they could be upset by what they were doing and still continue. I can imagine wanting to be reassured that the experiment was necessary, that the man conducting it knew what he was doing, that stopping would ruin important work. I would want him to take responsibility. But I would still be the person operating the switches.</p>\n<p>I used to reserve that kind of surrender for religious believers. They had accepted an authority I didn&#39;t accept, which seemed sufficient evidence that I was less susceptible. I hadn&#39;t applied the same test to my own confidence. Calling believers gullible had also allowed me to think well of myself, a reward I had been quite willing to accept without examining whether my own beliefs deserved the confidence I placed in them.</p>\n<p>So when I read about possession now, I can understand it as the loss of a person&#39;s judgement to something they have allowed to govern them. Their intelligence may remain intact and become very busy defending the commitment. An objection gets examined for signs of hostility before it is examined for truth. The person still reasons, but certain answers have become unacceptable because accepting them would cost too much.</p>\n<p>Religion hasn&#39;t become harmless to me because I can find this understanding in it. Christians can read warnings about false prophets and still follow one. I can read about Babylon and congratulate myself on recognising the corruption of people I already dislike. The story is available for that use too.</p>\n<p>But the merchants keep me from finding it entirely comfortable. Their grief is so intelligible, because they have lost the source of their prosperity and are appalled, while the people sold as part of that prosperity were already suffering when business was good. Reading the account, I want to believe I would have cared about them before the ships stopped coming.</p>\n";

